---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { HEAD_REPO_URL, REPO_URL, href } from '../config';
import * as s from '../lib/snippets';
import { rfcSections } from '../lib/markdown';

const sec = rfcSections();
const rfc = (n: string) => `${href('/rfc/')}#${sec.get(n)}`;
---
<Base title="Producers" path="/producers/" description="Send a first card to a head with curl, with @pbcp/client, or through the MCP server, and read the decision back.">
  <h1>Producers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A producer is anything that sends cards: an agent, a script, a mail reader. It needs an HTTP client, a token,
    and somewhere to keep one number.
  </p>
  <p>
    The snippets below run as written against a head once two variables are set. The shell ones need
    <code>curl</code> and <code>jq</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.env}</code></pre>

  <h2>1. Read the owner</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /</code> describes the head (<a href={rfc('7.5')}>§7.5</a>). Its <code>owner</code> is the person the head
    stores cards for, and every card you send is addressed to it in <code>to_person</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.describe}</code></pre>
  <p class="muted">A head may require the producer token for <code>GET /</code>; mint the token first (step 2) and add <code>-H "Authorization: Bearer $PBCP_TOKEN"</code> if yours does.</p>

  <h2>2. Get a token</h2>
  <p>
    Each producer holds a bearer token, issued by the head and bound to one namespace root: every id it sends
    starts with that root (<a href={rfc('12')}>§12</a>). How a head issues tokens is up to the head. On the
    <a href={HEAD_REPO_URL}>reference head</a>, for the root <code>demo</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.mint}</code></pre>
  <p>Set <code>PBCP_TOKEN</code> to the printed value.</p>

  <h2>3. Send a card</h2>
  <p>
    Submit is a <code>PUT</code> to the card's id (<a href={rfc('7.1')}>§7.1</a>). The id is
    <code>root:namespace:key</code>, minted by you, so the same event always gives the same id
    (<a href={rfc('5')}>§5</a>).
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.put}</code></pre>
  <p>The head answers <code>201</code> with the stored card, now carrying the fields it owns: <code>state</code>, <code>change_seq</code> and <code>delivery</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>{s.putAgain}</code></pre>

  <h2>4. Read the decision</h2>
  <p>
    The person decides in the head. You read decisions by syncing (<a href={rfc('7.2')}>§7.2</a>): every card in your
    namespace that changed after a cursor, in order. Start at <code>0</code>, or at the head's <code>seq</code> from
    step 1 to see only what happens from now on.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.sync}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{s.syncNext}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Act on a decision in a way that is safe to repeat, and move the cursor only after you have: the same change can
    reach you twice.
  </p>

  <h2>With @pbcp/client</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/client`}><code>@pbcp/client</code></a> wraps the same three calls, builds
    ids, and checks a card before it is sent. Its <code>runProducer</code> is the loop above with a durable cursor.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.clientInstall}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{s.client}</code></pre>

  <h2>With the MCP server</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/mcp`}><code>@pbcp/mcp</code></a> gives an agent the producer's side as
    tools: <code>submit_card</code>, <code>poll_changes</code> and, on a head with orders, <code>claim_order</code>. It
    holds a producer token, so it cannot decide.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{s.mcpRun}</code></pre>
  <p>As an MCP server entry in a client's configuration:</p>
  <pre><code>{s.mcpConfig}</code></pre>
  <p>The input for <code>submit_card</code>, with your owner id in <code>to_person</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{s.mcpSubmit}</code></pre>
  <p>and for <code>poll_changes</code>; call it again with the returned <code>next</code> until it stops moving:</p>
  <pre><code>{s.mcpPoll}</code></pre>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={rfc('11.1')}>§11.1</a>, the producer checklist.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/connectors/')}>Connectors</a>, complete producers to read or copy.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/rfc/')}>The RFC</a>, for threads, supersession and urgency.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
